# <stack> — audit checklist addon (template)

> One file per stack in the repo, sitting next to `shared.md`. Loaded by `/audit` when
> the diff touches files that map to this stack, or explicitly via `/audit <stack>`.
>
> Fill this from the architect intake (`ARCHITECT-INTAKE.md`), not from a style guide.
> Anything a linter already enforces does not belong here.

## Scope

Which paths map to this stack, and the framework plus version they run:

- `<repo>/<glob>` — <framework, version, any fork or unusual variant>

If you run a fork or a pinned old major, say so on the first line. It changes what
every other rule means.

## Layout and request flow (section 2)

Entry point → bootstrap → routing → handler → service → output. Name the real files.
Where a second registration step exists, say it here rather than trusting anyone to
remember.

## Code and style (section 3)

Only what a linter cannot catch. Naming the router requires, legacy patterns to match
rather than "fix", constructs that are banned and why.

## Placement and config (section 4)

Where a new file of each kind goes. Which config file needs a matching entry when you
add something. Anything that must be edited in two places.

## Security (section 5)

Stack-specific injection and escaping paths, mass-assignment surfaces, auth decorators
or route groups that must be present, constant-time comparison requirements.

## Performance (section 7)

The N+1 shapes this stack makes easy. Anything with a deadline or timeout, and what
happens when it is exceeded.

## Tests (section 8)

Test namespaces and filters. The command that runs a useful subset. What the full suite
covers and what it silently does not.

## DB and migrations (section 9)

Migration location and naming. Whether they must be reversible. Codegen or schema
regeneration that must follow a model change. Guards on destructive operations.

## Docs, CI and shipping (section 10)

Exact pre-ship commands, in order:

```bash
<lint>
<type-check>
<test>
<build>
```

Note any target that does more than its name suggests — a lint step that also tidies
dependencies will fail on an untidied new dependency, and the error will not say so.

---

## Rule format

Keep each rule on one line where possible, and make it decidable:

```
- <what to check>. -> <what to do instead>.        [section N] [mechanical|judgment]
```

`mechanical` rules get fixed in place and reported as `FIXED`. `judgment` rules are
reported as `OPEN` for a human. A rule that declares neither will be guessed at.
